<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · 1784 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Plaisir d'amour</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French love song written in 1784. Its tune was later used for Elvis Presley's &quot;Can't Help Falling in Love&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Jean-Pierre Claris de Florian</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Jean-Paul-Égide Martini</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French art song of the late 18th century</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1784</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Jean-Pierre Claris de Florian (he died in 1794); music by Jean-Paul-Égide Martini (1784; he died in 1816))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaisir-damour">Plaisir d'amour</a>&quot; (&quot;The Pleasure of Love&quot;) is a French love song written in 1784 by the composer Jean-Paul-Égide Martini (1741–1816). Its words come from a poem by Jean-Pierre Claris de Florian (1755–1794), which appears in his novel Célestine.</p><p>Martini's song was a great success. In 1816 a young woman, Julie Charles, sang it to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a> while he was taking a cure at Aix-les-Bains, and Lamartine recalled it thirty years later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Hector Berlioz</a> arranged it for orchestra in 1859, and in the 1880s Louis van Waefelghem arranged the tune for viola d'amore or viola and piano.</p><p>In the 20th century it was recorded by singers of many kinds, among them Yvonne Printemps (1931), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> (1940), Joan Baez (1961) and Mary Hopkin, in Welsh, in 1971. It was a theme of the films Love Affair (1939) and The Heiress (1949). Its melody was the basis of Elvis Presley's &quot;Can't Help Falling in Love&quot; (1961) and of the 20th-century Christian hymn &quot;My God Loves Me&quot;. In his short story &quot;Chagrin d'amour&quot; (1908), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-hesse">Hermann Hesse</a> invented a medieval origin for it, giving it to an obscure troubadour who sings it to a queen he loves without hope.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of lost love, tender and sorrowful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Plaisir d’amour ne dure qu’un moment,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The pleasure of love lasts only a moment,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chagrin d’amour dure toute la vie.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sorrow of love lasts all one's life.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">J’ai tout quitté pour l’ingrate Sylvie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I gave up everything for ungrateful Sylvie;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Elle me quitte et prend un autre amant.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>she leaves me and takes another lover.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Plaisir d’amour ne dure qu’un moment,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The pleasure of love lasts only a moment,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chagrin d’amour dure toute la vie.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sorrow of love lasts all one's life.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tant que cette eau coulera doucement</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;As long as this water flows gently</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Vers ce ruisseau qui borde la prairie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>toward the stream that borders the meadow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Je t’aimerai, me répétait Sylvie…</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will love you,&quot; Sylvie kept telling me…</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’eau coule encor, elle a changé pourtant !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The water still flows, yet she has changed!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Plaisir d’amour ne dure qu’un moment,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The pleasure of love lasts only a moment,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chagrin d’amour dure toute la vie.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sorrow of love lasts all one's life.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Jean-Pierre Claris de Florian (1784), as printed in La Chanson française du XVe au XXe siècle. Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/La_Chanson_fran%C3%A7aise_du_XVe_au_XXe_si%C3%A8cle%2FPlaisir_d%E2%80%99amour" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>The English follows a translation by the Institute's editor, fitted to the lines as printed.</li><li>The poem is a song in Florian's novella Célestine (1784).</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="Q05TgSgjdEY" data-title="Plaisir d'amour: The King's Singers"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The King's Singers</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The King's Singers - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q05TgSgjdEY" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for orchestra, H134 (Hector Berlioz)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1859</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for viola d'amore or viola and piano (Louis van Waefelghem)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chagrin d'amour (Hermann Hesse)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1908</span><span class="sg-chain-h">short story giving the song a fictional origin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Plaisir d'amour (Rina Ketty)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording with extended lyrics</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Love Affair (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1939</span><span class="sg-chain-h">theme of the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Heiress (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1949</span><span class="sg-chain-h">main theme, sung by Montgomery Clift</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ma France bien-aimée, in We're No Angels (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1955</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Can't Help Falling in Love (Elvis Presley)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody based on the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My God Loves Me (hymn)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody based on the song</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Pleserau Serch (Mary Hopkin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Welsh version</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Poem in the novel Célestine (Jean-Pierre Claris de Florian)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the words</span></li></ul></section>
